rafasaurus's dotfiles
---------------------

Uses GNU Stow: https://www.gnu.org/software/stow/

Supported on Arch Linux. Install GNU Stow before continuing. Some targets also
require `sudo`, `pacman`, `paru`, Docker, and the repository submodules.

Usage:
```
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/rafasaurus/dotfiles --recursive
cd dotfiles
make stow
```

`make stow` applies the `common` package and, when present, the package named
by `hostname` (for example, `x220`).

Use `make help` to list available targets. `install-udev` writes privileged
system rules and `uninstall-udev` removes only this repository's rule filenames.
Review those targets before running them. `make stow` may replace an existing
`~/.config/mimeapps.list`.

Security and setup:
- Install Neovim from Arch packages (`neovim` is in `packages.txt`); there is no
  AppImage downloader. Existing manually installed binaries are not removed.
- Run `make install-vim-plug` to install the pinned, SHA-256-verified plugin
  manager for Vim and Neovim. Editor startup never downloads it. Run
  `:PlugInstall` explicitly; plugins and AUR/source builds remain trusted code
  that you should review before installing/updating.
- Arduino/serial and ST-Link tools use the Arch `uucp` group, not world-writable
  devices. Add your user once with `sudo usermod -aG uucp "$USER"`, then log out
  and back in. Run `make install-udev` and reconnect devices to apply the rules.
- Plain suspend intentionally does not lock. Use `suspend_and_lock` when wanted.
- QR clipboard output stays at `/tmp/qr.png` until the viewer exits, then is
  shredded and removed (also on handled signals/errors). Existing paths are
  refused. Shredding cannot guarantee erasure on SSDs, tmpfs or COW filesystems.
- Run `make test-security` for isolated regressions (requires Python 3, a C
  compiler, Zsh and GNU coreutils; no root, network or desktop access needed).

Text login (greetd + tuigreet):
```
make install-login
# Save your work, then reboot:
systemctl reboot
```

Disable any other display manager first. Use the tty1 login instead of startw;
dwl exits or crashes return to authentication. Log out of other TTY shells.
Logs: `journalctl -t dwl -b`. Recovery: Ctrl+Alt+F2, log in, run
`sudo systemctl disable greetd`, then reboot.
